<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- One of the two firewall helpers - the fourth category under src/, next to the libraries,
       the applications and the platform project.

       Why an executable of its own rather than a mode of one program, or a call inside the
       control: THE UAC PROMPT NAMES THE PROGRAM. A prompt that reads
       DnDOverlay.FirewallRemove.exe tells the person the moment rights are being asked for what
       is about to happen; a helper with two modes shows "something to do with the firewall", and
       a library call inside the control shows nothing at all. That is exactly the moment trust is
       being asked for, so it is not a cosmetic point (Part 9).

       Neither helper takes arguments. A program that starts elevated and lets an unprivileged
       caller tell it what to do is a privilege escalation with a covering letter - so what is to
       be done is decided in the program, from its own directory.

       No project reference, deliberately: it is one self-contained executable, and what the two
       share is a linked source file. Windows-bound in the target framework because netsh and the
       install path are, which is also what keeps it out of the Linux filter (Part 2). -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>false</UseWPF>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>DnDOverlay.Firewall</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>DnDOverlay.FirewallAdd</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\DnDOverlay.Firewall.Shared\FirewallRule.cs" Link="FirewallRule.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
